WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.460
Film Courage: Well I like how you say you
watch DRIFTER (that’s who you were at that

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.150
time) and maybe whether it makes you uncomfortable
or not, you see who you were, what you were

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:12.820
going through and what you were trying to
get the audience to feel.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:17.320
And you can see that with bands (let’s say
U2), their first two albums very angry and

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:23.130
raw (excellent albums) but different from
newer ones where life is maybe a little better,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:27.060
money is little more plentiful and so people
are in a different state of mind.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.300
But I get it, I was watching this talk show
with Wes Craven and I think it was from 1988

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:36.480
and it had some of the commercials still playing
[in the Youtube video] and this was from a

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.219
talk show that I watched as a little girl
and it was like “Wow?

00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:41.290
Even the tone of the commercials were more
upbeat?

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:43.059
The music, everything.”

00:00:43.059 --> 00:00:46.390
So the tonality of the world was different
then.

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:48.070
We reflect what we see.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:51.020
So in 2015, people had come out of the recession.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:58.280
It was a tough time for everyone especially
millennials because jobs were not, you could

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.469
have felt that.

00:00:59.469 --> 00:01:00.890
It was an angry time for a lot of people.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:07.810
Chris von Hoffmann, Horror Writer/Director:
I think I was just open to exposing the ugliness

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:09.630
of humanity.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:15.130
The flip side of things like Bret Easton Ellis.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:18.580
When he wrote THE RULES OF ATTRACTION and
AMERICAN PSYCHO and all of these kind of things.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:24.180
He was much more attracted to the flaws of
people.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.070
And I think the one thing that I’m very
attracted to is kind of like a common thing

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.270
that no matter how mainstream I would want
to dabble in.

00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:37.170
I am very interested in very flawed people
and very flawed characters against even more

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:38.470
flawed characters.

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:47.190
Sort of like a somewhat cynical outlook on
the world but in a fun, exciting way.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:51.810
Not in a witty kind of entertaining kind of
way.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:59.990
But I think I’m getting better at approaching
it with the same kind of intention but I mean

00:01:59.990 --> 00:02:04.840
lighter is not the best word but a little
more audience friendly but still having that

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.640
same kind of outlook.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:10.979
Because I don’t want to loose that kind
of outlook because I think that’s what make

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:19.480
my kind of stuff specific, something a little
more like my mom would enjoy.

00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:21.590
DRIFTER is not her kind of movie.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.800
It’s just not her kind of movie.

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:30.110
Film Courage: With DRIFTER I’m not sure
if you’re able to talk about the budget?

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:33.580
I was curious how you did the location scouting
because you talked about the desert.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.730
And how you were very intrigued by the desert.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:37.430
I know the desert has always intrigued me.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:42.230
There is something frightening about it and
then something mysterious and etherial about

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:46.849
it because you can kind of get lost in there
and there are all these interesting drifters

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:51.600
(there really are and you kind of wonder what
their background is).

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.620
What is it about the desert and how did you
find the location?

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.000
Chris: Well we shot the movie for like $40,000.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:07.950
But I’d shot a couple shorts in the Lancaster
area/Palmdale so I was a little familiar with

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:09.860
that area.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:14.330
And DRIFTER was the first time I really embraced
my fascination with the desert because I had

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:23.150
been really fascinated with it for a couple
of years because I love stories about isolation.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.340
You know just getting old before your time
and just burning so much gas money just driving

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.450
to the outskirts because there was no location
manager or anything.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:34.790
It was a very skeleton crew.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:40.420
But the kicking off point was that my cinematographer
had known about the town that was in Bombay

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.730
Beach.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:47.299
BAD MATCH with Keanu Reeves shot there a few
years ago, they had wrapped filming a few

00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:52.290
months before we had arrived.

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:55.920
But I think mostly they do photoshoots and
commercials there and they don’t do that

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.720
many features (maybe they are starting to).

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:08.430
But that was the kicking off point to write
a story around that town and then drive around

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.349
all over the place trying to find something.

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:16.940
It was actually really…since there was no
location manager that movie is so much driven

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:21.169
by the set pieces, the locations were paramount
in that story.

00:04:21.169 --> 00:04:26.770
If the location didn’t fit, that movie was
going to get laughed off the screen and I’ve

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:33.660
seen a lot of micro-budget, post-apocalyptic
stories shooting in Vasquez Rocks in Valencia

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:38.340
[a popular LA County attraction] and you see
tourists in the background.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:43.330
I want to shoot in places that no one shoots
in, forbidden places.

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:44.330
Film Courage: Devil’s Punchbowl.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:45.330
Chris: Yeah.

00:04:45.330 --> 00:04:50.840
I wanted to go to Death Valley but it was
way too far.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:56.270
So just finding these different places and
we were in pre-production for a long time.

00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:06.130
It was a very small crew so I need time to
get all that stuff.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:11.810
And the locations were probably the most major
thing because you don’t want to break the

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:15.150
bank but you want to make sure that the locations
are as cinematic as you can get.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:21.050
So a lot of negotiating and shooting and random
spots all over the place.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:29.050
But it was mainly all around Lancaster, Joshua
Tree, Bombay Beach, Victorville.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:35.979
We shot a little in LA inside this haunted
house, only enough right around the corner

00:05:35.979 --> 00:05:37.139
from where I lived.

00:05:37.139 --> 00:05:42.740
I’d been trying to find that location since
forever and I would go in all these houses

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:48.050
that were normal houses.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:57.490
I was trying to look for a haunted manor,
TALES FROM THE CRYPT kind of house.

00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:02.120
And I was shocked that we were able to get
the house that we got because the guy was

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:08.630
just this super nice guy and hooked us up
on a great deal with it.

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:16.960
I just get so obsessive because I love locations,
I love the bigger the better.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:21.950
And seeing this house that was right around
the corner, right around my nose the entire

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:29.199
time and it literally had everything we needed.

00:06:29.199 --> 00:06:34.360
All the rooms they had extra furniture, literally
everything we needed was there.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:40.980
It was very odd, almost like destiny.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:48.789
But locations, that’s one of the things
that I’ve very stubborn about in pre-production

00:06:48.789 --> 00:06:55.620
because it dictates the quality of the movie
and I’ve seen films with really crappy locations

00:06:55.620 --> 00:07:01.170
and I’m just like “Why would you ever
use that?”

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:09.050
You’ve got to be realistic at a certain
point.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:17.009
But I think there’s a difference between
being realistic and settling.

00:07:17.009 --> 00:07:22.770
You want to make sure you can get the best
location you can possibly get.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:25.240
And I think a lot of people are just like
“That’s okay.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:26.500
Let’s just shoot there.

00:07:26.500 --> 00:07:31.650
We’ll just shoot around it.”

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:33.210
Locations are so, so important.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:38.500
If you’re making a cinematic story, they
are very, very important.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:41.949
It just dictates so many things.

00:07:41.949 --> 00:07:45.160
Film Courage: How did you deal with the wind
and the heat?

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:51.470
And also too I don’t know how close were
any stores, so how were you dealing with the

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:56.090
remote location and the elements?

00:07:56.090 --> 00:08:01.900
Chris: We shot two filming blocks in July
and August 2015 and in July we were shooting

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:07.569
a lot of night stuff in the Lancaster/Victorville
area and it was freezing.

00:08:07.569 --> 00:08:13.210
One girl she was covered in fake blood for
hours that night and she was shivering (her

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:14.790
teeth were chattering the entire time).

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:23.830
It was rough but that was nothing compared
to when we shot in the town because the Film

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:28.110
Commission, they were like laughing at us
because we were shooting in August.

00:08:28.110 --> 00:08:35.659
Even that other movie that I was talking about,
they filmed in April and even then it’s

00:08:35.659 --> 00:08:39.260
hot out there and it doesn’t start to cool
until October.

00:08:39.260 --> 00:08:40.279
We could push until October.

00:08:40.279 --> 00:08:45.010
I wasn’t going to be one of these guys who
shoots on the weekend and months later shoots

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:46.010
again.

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:49.110
I’m too impatient for that.

00:08:49.110 --> 00:08:56.660
And so in the thick of August, literally the
worst time you were supposed to be shooting

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:57.660
there.

00:08:57.660 --> 00:09:02.100
I remember we were so cautious if we could
afford a medic there because we needed a medic

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:04.070
to come about and we were nervous if we could
afford it.

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:10.110
And then we finally did get one to come out
and there were a couple moments where people

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.490
almost passed out.

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:18.600
And it was very intense.

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.080
I’d never felt heat like that before.

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:25.310
I remember when we first arrived, everyone
was having a great time driving out there.

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:29.160
And the moment we first arrived people couldn’t
breathe when we stepped out of the car because

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:37.800
the moment we arrived we went right into filming
and people were already feeling it.

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:42.930
And we had four more much more ambitious days
after that.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:48.540
I think the hottest it got was 120 [degrees]
and it was the biggest day that it was the

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:51.350
hottest (the most ambitious day).

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:56.840
But I think looking back at it, I think it
dictated people’s performances.

00:09:56.840 --> 00:10:02.260
I think it really…freezing cold and scorching
heat I think it really influenced people’s

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:08.000
performances because all that sweat is all
real.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:14.620
The sunburn and all of that, all of it is
real.

00:10:14.620 --> 00:10:20.610
I just love the stories of the original TEXAS
CHAINSAW MASSACRE, scorching heat and such

00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:27.589
a nasty atmosphere and locations they were
filming in so that you don’t want to touch

00:10:27.589 --> 00:10:28.589
anything.

00:10:28.589 --> 00:10:33.110
You look at the film and it lends itself very
well to it.

